Session 2, 20.10.2026

Today we continued exploring electronic components and circuit configurations. We looked closer at how resistors work, 
using the color code bands (like 4-band or 5-band codes) to read their values, and discussed surface mount devices (SMD).
We also learned more about power calculations and circuit types:

- Ohm's Law: V = I × R (Voltage equals current times resistance)
- Power Law: P = I × V (Power equals current times voltage)

Although I have already heard about the differences between each, I haven't really looked further into it beforehand.
So this was new to me.

Circuits: We compared series circuits (where components share the same line and divide voltage) and 
parallel circuits (where every device receives full source voltage independently). 
Here I started to understand how it power works.

We experimented with powering the breadboard using an external power supply module and different LEDs. 
Then we also noticed that different colored LEDs (like green) require different forward voltages and energy levels. 
This was very interesting to see, I had to cover my eyes because some LEDs were shining so bright.

Also we wired up an RGB LED using three separate resistors and learned how to mix colors using (analogWrite()) and duty cycles.
I made purple and although I had some struggles at first with my code, I managed to finish it. 

For defining pins as variables and controlling multiple LEDs, we used code structures like this:

const int redLED = 2;

void setup() {
  pinMode(redLED, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(redLED, HIGH);
  delay(1000);
  digitalWrite(redLED, LOW);
  delay(1000);
}


And for the RGB LED color-mixing function we looked at:

void setRGB (int redValue, int greenValue, int blueValue) {
  analogWrite(9, redValue);
  analogWrite(10, greenValue);
  analogWrite(11, blueValue);
}

